  "slug": "colored-indexes-832d",
  "summary": "Two apo-to-holo flow-matching models and RNA design methods using verifier feedback or probing data show structure-conditioned generation moving toward physical fidelity.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is generative modeling conditioned on explicit structural representations. Apo-to-holo conformational transition serves as a shared testbed: CrypticFlow combines Cartesian C-alpha anchors with internal-coordinate midpoints in hierarchical flow matching, while A2Holo runs dual-stream flow matching over protein language model embeddings and 3D structure. For RNA, KnitNet adds learned inference-time search driven by verifier feedback for exact inverse design, BRIDGE pretrains an inverse-folding encoder on backbone atoms from 2,501 structures, and MXfold2 learns from chemical probing through implicit maximum-likelihood gradients across discrete folding. Predictive work on catalytic residues, binding affinity, ΔΔG and allostery runs alongside; the allostery study notably uses language model attention without structure. The common novelty is coupling learned models to physical or verifiable structural constraints.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
